    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I'm working on a digital collection of 19th-century cartographic materials and I'm running into some issues with Dublin Core implementation. Specifically, when we have maps where the creator or contributor is completely unidentifiable or the attribution is highly ambiguous, what are the best practices for handling those fields to maintain archival integrity without leaving them blank?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In Dublin Core, you can use xsi:nil=\"true\" to explicitly indicate missing data rather than leaving fields blank, which prevents semantic errors during metadata interoperability. Additionally, for ambiguous or unidentifiable creators, consider using a value of \"unknown\" within a controlled vocabulary or adding a separate descriptive note field to document the investigative process behind the attribution. This approach adheres to ORE (Open Recent Events) best practices, ensuring that the record remains rich with all known information while clearly signaling where certainty is lacking.",
